```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

Confidence intervals for
semipartial correlations,
squared semipartial correlations,
squared partial correlations,
the improvement in R-squared,
and Cohen's f-squared
are generated using
the `SCorSandwich()`, `SCorSqSandwich()`, `PCorSandwich()`, `DeltaRSqSandwich()`,
and `FSqSandwich()` functions from the `betaSandwich` package.
In this example,
we use the data set and the model used in
[betaSandwich: Example Using the BetaHC Function](example-beta-sandwich.html).

```{r}
#| message = FALSE
library(betaSandwich)
```

```{r}
#| echo = FALSE
if (!exists("nas1982")) {
  try(
    data(
      "nas1982",
      package = "betaSandwich"
    ),
    silent = TRUE
  )
}
df <- nas1982
```

```{r}
#| eval = FALSE
df <- betaSandwich::nas1982
```

## Fit the regression model using the `lm()` function.

```{r}
object <- lm(QUALITY ~ NARTIC + PCTGRT + PCTSUPP, data = df)
```

## Estimate the standardized regression slopes and the corresponding sampling covariance matrix.

#### Multivariate Normal-Theory Approach

```{r}
std_mvn <- BetaN(object)
```

#### Asymptotic Distribution-Free Approach

```{r}
std_adf <- BetaADF(object)
```

#### HC3

```{r}
std_hc3 <- BetaHC(object, type = "hc3")
```

## Semipartial correlations

The semipartial correlation of regressor $j$ is
$sr_{j} = \beta^{\ast}_{j} / \sqrt{\left[ \mathbf{R}_{x}^{-1} \right]_{jj}}$,
the correlation between the outcome and the part of regressor $j$
that is not explained by the other regressors.

```{r}
summary(SCorSandwich(std_hc3, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Squared semipartial correlations

The `SCorSqSandwich()` function squares the signed-semipartial interval as a set.
If the signed interval contains zero,
the lower limit of the squared interval is zero.
Writing the signed interval as $\left[ l, u \right]$,
the squared interval is $\left[ l^{2}, u^{2} \right]$ if $0 < l$,
$\left[ u^{2}, l^{2} \right]$ if $u < 0$,
and $\left[ 0, \max \left( l^{2}, u^{2} \right) \right]$ otherwise.
The function returns a data frame with the estimate and the confidence limits.

```{r}
SCorSqSandwich(std_hc3, level = 0.95)
```

## Squared partial correlations

The squared partial correlation is
$pr^{2}_{j} = sr^{2}_{j} / \left( 1 - R^{2} + sr^{2}_{j} \right)$,
the proportion of the variance of the outcome
not explained by the other regressors
that is explained by regressor $j$.

```{r}
summary(PCorSandwich(std_hc3, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Improvement in R-squared

The improvement in R-squared is
$\Delta R^{2}_{j} = R^{2} - R^{2}_{-j} = sr^{2}_{j}$,
the decrease in $R^{2}$ when regressor $j$ is removed from the model.

```{r}
summary(DeltaRSqSandwich(std_hc3, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Cohen's f-squared and local f-squared

Cohen's f-squared is
$f^{2} = R^{2} / \left( 1 - R^{2} \right)$
and the local f-squared of regressor $j$ is
$f^{2}_{j} = sr^{2}_{j} / \left( 1 - R^{2} \right)$.
By convention, values of about 0.02, 0.15, and 0.35
are small, medium, and large effects.

```{r}
summary(FSqSandwich(std_hc3, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Extracting estimates, sampling covariance matrices, and confidence intervals

The `coef()`, `vcov()`, and `confint()` methods
work on the objects returned by the effect size functions.

```{r}
scor <- SCorSandwich(std_hc3)
coef(scor)
vcov(scor)
confint(scor, level = 0.95)
```

## Comparing covariance estimators

```{r}
confint(DeltaRSqSandwich(std_mvn), level = 0.95)
confint(DeltaRSqSandwich(std_adf), level = 0.95)
confint(DeltaRSqSandwich(std_hc3), level = 0.95)
```

